'use strict';
MANUAL.add('expand', [
  {
    name: 'expansions', kind: 'concept', aliases: ['shell expansion', 'shell expansions', 'order of expansion', 'expansion order', 'expansions: the order', 'expansion', 'how the shell reads a line', 'tokens', 'metacharacters', 'shell parsing'],
    summary: 'What the shell does to a command line before running it, and in which order.',
    synopsis: ['echo {a,b}$HOME/$(date +%Y)*.txt "$((1+2))"'],
    desc: [
      'Before a command runs, the **shell** reads the line and transforms it. The command never sees the original text, only the result. That is why `echo *` lists files and why quoting matters: quotes control *which* of these transformations happen.',
      'The order (bash) is: **1. brace expansion** (`{a,b}`) → **2. tilde expansion** (`~`) → **3. parameter and variable expansion** (`$var`, `${var:-x}`), **command substitution** (`$(cmd)`), **arithmetic expansion** (`$((1+2))`) — all at the same level, left to right → **4. word splitting** (of the *unquoted* results of step 3, using `$IFS`) → **5. pathname expansion** (globbing: `*`, `?`, `[ ]`) → **6. quote removal**. Redirections are processed after the expansions of the words that are their targets.',
      'Consequences you must know: braces are expanded **before** variables (`{1..$n}` does **not** work); the results of variable expansion are **split into words and globbed** unless the expansion is inside **double quotes** (the reason to write `"$var"`); and single quotes switch every one of these off.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'The steps in order', table: [
        ['#', 'Expansion', 'Example', 'Result'],
        ['1', 'brace', '`echo {a,b}.txt`', '`a.txt b.txt`'],
        ['2', 'tilde', '`echo ~`', '`/home/user`'],
        ['3', 'parameter / variable', '`echo $USER ${HOME}`', 'the values'],
        ['3', 'command substitution', '`echo $(date +%Y)`', 'the output of the command'],
        ['3', 'arithmetic', '`echo $((2+3))`', '`5`'],
        ['4', 'word splitting', '`x="a b"; ls $x`', 'two words: `a` and `b`'],
        ['5', 'pathname (glob)', '`echo *.txt`', 'matching file names'],
        ['6', 'quote removal', '`echo "a" \'b\'`', '`a b`'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'All the steps in one line', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nname=report\necho {x,y}-$name-$((1+1)) *.txt ~ "~"', note: 'The brace made two words, `$name` and the arithmetic were replaced, `*.txt` became the two file names, the tilde became the home directory and the quoted one stayed literal.' },
      { title: 'The command sees the result, not the pattern', cmd: 'touch a b c\nset -x; echo *; set +x', note: '`set -x` shows the line after expansion (the lines starting with `+`).' },
      { title: 'Braces are expanded before variables', cmd: 'n=3\necho {1..3}\necho {1..$n}\neval echo {1..$n}', note: 'The second does not work; `seq 1 $n` or `eval` does.' },
      { title: 'Unquoted variables are split and globbed', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nv="*.txt"\necho $v\necho "$v"', note: 'The unquoted `$v` became the file names; the quoted one stays `*.txt`.' },
      { title: 'Quotes switch things off', cmd: 'echo "$HOME"   \'$HOME\'   \\$HOME   "*"', note: 'In double quotes `$` still works; in single quotes nothing does; a backslash protects one character.' },
    ],
    see: ['quoting', 'parameter-expansion', 'command-substitution', 'brace-expansion', 'glob', 'word-splitting', 'arithmetic'],
  },
  {
    name: 'variables', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['variable', 'shell variables', 'environment variables', 'assignment', 'VAR=value', '$VAR', '${VAR}', '$var', 'variable assignment', '${var}', 'variables de entorno', 'shell vs environment variables', 'variable naming', 'PATH', 'HOME', 'USER', 'PWD', 'OLDPWD', 'SHELL', 'LANG', 'HOSTNAME', 'RANDOM', 'SECONDS', 'LINENO', 'UID', 'EUID', 'PS1', 'BASH_VERSION',  'TERM', 'EDITOR', 'TMPDIR', 'REPLY', 'OPTIND', 'OPTARG', 'BASH_REMATCH', 'FUNCNAME', 'HISTSIZE', 'HISTFILE', 'MANPATH', 'PS2', 'PROMPT_COMMAND', 'LC_ALL', 'path variable', 'variable names', 'readonly variable'],
    summary: 'Shell variables: assigning (`name=value`), using (`$name`, `${name}`), scope, environment variables and the important predefined ones.',
    synopsis: ['NAME=value            # assignment: NO spaces around =', 'echo "$NAME"  echo "${NAME}text"', 'NAME=value COMMAND     # for one command only', 'export NAME           # visible to child processes'],
    desc: [
      'A **variable** is a name that holds text. You create or change one with **`name=value`** — **no spaces around `=`** (`name = value` runs a command called `name`). Names use letters, digits and `_` and cannot start with a digit; by convention constants and environment variables are UPPER case, your own locals lower case. You read it by putting **`$`** in front: `echo $name`. Use **`${name}`** when the next character could be part of the name (`${name}_file`, `${n}th`). **Everything is a string**; arithmetic is done with `$(( ))`. An unset variable expands to the empty string (unless `set -u`).',
      'A variable you assign is a **shell variable**: visible only in the current shell. **`export name`** (or `export name=value`) marks it as an **environment variable**: every command and child process started from now on receives a *copy*. The copy cannot change the parent\'s value, so a script cannot change the variables of the shell that launched it (unless it is run with `source`). A one-off value for one command: `LC_ALL=C sort file` (not exported afterwards).',
      '**Quote your expansions**: `"$name"`. Unquoted, the value is split at blanks and treated as a glob pattern; a value with a space or `*` then breaks the command. Assigning does not need quotes for the right-hand side word splitting (`a=$b` is safe), but values with spaces do (`a="x y"`).',
      '**Command-line "arguments" are variables too**: `$1`, `$2`... `$#`, `$@`, `$?` (see `special-parameters`).',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'Predefined variables worth knowing', table: [
        ['Variable', 'Holds'],
        ['`HOME`', 'your home directory (`~` expands to it)'],
        ['`PATH`', 'colon-separated list of directories searched for commands'],
        ['`PWD` / `OLDPWD`', 'current / previous directory (`cd` maintains them)'],
        ['`USER` / `LOGNAME`', 'login name'],
        ['`SHELL`', 'the user\'s login shell'],
        ['`UID` / `EUID`', 'real / effective numeric user id (bash)'],
        ['`HOSTNAME`', 'the machine name'],
        ['`LANG`, `LC_ALL`', 'language/locale (affects sorting, messages, character classes)'],
        ['`PS1`', 'the primary prompt'],
        ['`IFS`', 'the word-splitting characters (default: space, tab, newline)'],
        ['`RANDOM`', 'a different random number 0-32767 on every use'],
        ['`SECONDS`', 'seconds since the shell started'],
        ['`LINENO`', 'current line number of the script'],
        ['`BASH_VERSION`', 'the version of bash'],
        ['`TERM`', 'terminal type'],
        ['`EDITOR`, `VISUAL`', 'preferred editor for programs that start one'],
        ['`TMPDIR`', 'directory for temporary files (used by `mktemp`)'],
        ['`PIPESTATUS`', 'array with the exit status of each command of the last pipeline'],
        ['`REPLY`', 'default variable of `read`'],
        ['`OPTIND`, `OPTARG`', 'used by `getopts`'],
        ['`BASH_REMATCH`', 'array with the groups of the last `[[ =~ ]]`'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Assign and use', cmd: 'name=Ana\necho "Hello, $name"\necho "Hello, ${name}!"' },
      { title: 'No spaces around the =', cmd: 'x = 5; echo "status: $?"\nx=5; echo "x=$x"', fails: true, note: 'With spaces bash tries to run a command named `x` with the arguments `=` and `5`.' },
      { title: 'Braces separate the name', cmd: 'file=report\necho "$file_2024"\necho "${file}_2024"', note: 'Without braces bash looks for a variable called `file_2024` (empty).' },
      { title: 'Values with spaces need quotes', cmd: 'greeting="hello world"\necho "$greeting"\ngreeting2=hello world; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Why quotes on expansions matter', cmd: 'touch "my file.txt"\nf="my file.txt"\nls $f; echo "status: $?"\nls "$f"', fails: true, note: 'Unquoted, `$f` becomes two words (`my` and `file.txt`).' },
      { title: 'Variables hold strings, math needs $(( ))', cmd: 'a=2; b=3\necho "$a + $b"\necho "$((a + b))"' },
      { title: 'Command output into a variable', cmd: 'count=$(printf "a\\nb\\nc\\n" | wc -l)\necho "lines: $count"' },
      { title: 'Shell variable vs environment variable', cmd: 'local_var=shell\nexport env_var=exported\nbash -c \'echo "child sees: [$local_var] [$env_var]"\'' },
      { title: 'A child cannot change the parent', cmd: 'export v=parent\nbash -c \'v=child; echo "in child: $v"\'\necho "in parent: $v"' },
      { title: 'One command only', cmd: 'x=outer\nx=temp bash -c \'echo "inside: $x"\'\necho "after: $x"' },
      { title: 'Predefined variables', cmd: 'echo "$HOME"\necho "$PWD"\necho "${PATH%%:*}"\necho "$SHELL"\necho "$UID"', note: '`${PATH%%:*}` is the first directory of the PATH.' },
      { title: 'RANDOM and SECONDS', cmd: 'echo $(( RANDOM % 10 + 1 )) | grep -cE "^([1-9]|10)$"\nSECONDS=0; sleep 1; echo "elapsed: $SECONDS"' },
      { title: 'See all variables', cmd: 'x=1\nset | grep "^x="\nenv | grep "^HOME="', note: '`set` lists shell variables and functions, `env` only the environment.' },
      { title: 'Unset variable = empty (and set -u)', cmd: 'echo "[$nothing]"\nset -u\n( echo "[$nothing]" ) 2>&1 | sed "s/line [0-9]*/line N/"', note: 'With `set -u` (nounset) using an undefined variable is an error.' },
      { title: 'Read-only', cmd: 'readonly PI=3.14\nPI=3\necho "status: $?"\necho "$PI"', fails: true },
      { title: 'PATH: where commands are found', cmd: 'mkdir bin; printf \'#!/bin/bash\\necho my tool\\n\' > bin/mytool; chmod +x bin/mytool\nmytool; echo "status: $?"\nPATH="$PWD/bin:$PATH"\nmytool', fails: true },
    ],
    notes: [
      'A variable set inside a pipeline, a `( ... )` subshell or a `$( ... )` is **lost** afterwards: they run in a subshell.',
      'Changing `PATH` only affects the current shell (and children if exported): put permanent changes in `~/.bashrc` / `~/.profile`.',
      '`unset` removes a variable; `readonly` and `declare -r` make it immutable.',
    ],
    see: ['export', 'unset', 'quoting', 'parameter-expansion', 'special-parameters', 'env', 'declare'],
  },
  {
    name: 'export', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['export -p', 'export -n', 'export VAR=value', 'export -f', 'exported', 'export PATH'],
    summary: 'Mark variables (or functions) to be passed to the commands and child processes the shell starts.',
    synopsis: ['export [-fn] [NAME[=VALUE]]...', 'export -p'],
    desc: ['A plain assignment creates a variable visible only to this shell. **`export`** puts it in the **environment**, which every child process receives a **copy** of at start-up; a script run from your shell sees exported variables only. The change is **one way**: a child cannot modify its parent\'s variables. You can export at assignment (`export NAME=value`) or later (`NAME=value; export NAME`). `export -n NAME` removes the export mark (the variable stays); `unset NAME` removes the variable; `export -p` lists everything exported (in a reusable format); `export -f func` exports a bash function.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-p', desc: 'List all exported variables as `declare -x` lines.' },
      { flag: '-n', desc: 'Remove the export property (the variable stays as a shell variable).' },
      { flag: '-f', desc: 'Export functions.' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Without export the child does not see it', cmd: 'colour=blue\nbash -c \'echo "child: [$colour]"\'\nexport colour\nbash -c \'echo "child: [$colour]"\'' },
      { title: 'Export and assign at once', cmd: 'export GREETING="hello there"\nbash -c \'echo "$GREETING"\'' },
      { title: 'Changes in the child do not come back', cmd: 'export n=1\nbash -c \'n=99; echo "child: $n"\'\necho "parent: $n"' },
      { title: 'Extend PATH for this session', cmd: 'mkdir tools; printf \'#!/bin/sh\\necho from tools\\n\' > tools/hi; chmod +x tools/hi\nexport PATH="$PWD/tools:$PATH"\nhi' },
      { title: 'See what is exported', cmd: 'export FOO=bar\nexport -p | grep "FOO"', note: '`export -p` prints `declare -x NAME="value"`.' },
      { title: 'Stop exporting', cmd: 'export V=1\nexport -n V\nbash -c \'echo "child: [$V]"\'\necho "shell: $V"' },
      { title: 'A script cannot export to its caller', cmd: 'printf \'export LEAKED=yes\\n\' > set.sh\nbash set.sh\necho "[$LEAKED]"\nsource set.sh\necho "[$LEAKED]"', note: 'The script runs in a child: its export dies with it. `source` (or `.`) runs it in the current shell.' },
      { title: 'Export a function', cmd: 'hello() { echo "hello from a function"; }\nexport -f hello\nbash -c hello' },
    ],
    see: ['variables', 'unset', 'env', 'declare', 'source'],
  },
  {
    name: 'unset', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['unset -v', 'unset -f', 'unset VAR', 'unset array element'],
    summary: 'Remove variables, functions or array elements.',
    synopsis: ['unset [-fv] NAME...'],
    desc: ['`unset NAME` deletes the variable (or the function with `-f`): afterwards it is **unset**, which is different from set to an empty string — `${NAME-default}` and `[[ -v NAME ]]` can tell them apart. Read-only variables cannot be unset. `unset arr[2]` removes one element of an array; `unset arr` removes the whole array. `unset` removes the variable from the **environment** too.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-v', desc: 'Treat each NAME as a variable (default).' },
      { flag: '-f', desc: 'Treat each NAME as a function.' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Remove a variable', cmd: 'x=5\necho "[$x]"\nunset x\necho "[$x]"' },
      { title: 'Unset is not the same as empty', cmd: 'a=""\nunset b\n[[ -v a ]] && echo "a is set (empty)"\n[[ -v b ]] || echo "b is unset"\necho "${a-default} ${b-default}"\necho "${a:-default} ${b:-default}"', note: '`-` substitutes only when unset; `:-` also when empty.' },
      { title: 'Remove a function', cmd: 'greet() { echo hi; }\ngreet\nunset -f greet\ngreet; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'An array element and a whole array', cmd: 'arr=(a b c d)\nunset "arr[1]"\necho "${arr[@]}" "(${#arr[@]} elements)"\nunset arr\necho "[${arr[@]}]"', note: 'Removing an element does not renumber the others.' },
      { title: 'Cannot unset a read-only variable', cmd: 'readonly R=1\nunset R; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Unset in a script before use', cmd: 'DEBUG=1\nunset DEBUG\n[ -z "${DEBUG+x}" ] && echo "DEBUG is not set"' },
      { title: 'It also leaves the environment', cmd: 'export E=1\nunset E\nbash -c \'echo "child: [$E]"\'' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 unless a NAME is read-only.'],
    see: ['variables', 'export', 'declare', 'parameter-expansion'],
  },
  {
    name: 'env', kind: 'command', aliases: ['printenv', 'env -i', 'env VAR=x cmd', 'env -u', 'env | grep', 'env -0', 'env -S', 'environment'],
    summary: 'Show the environment, or run a command with a modified environment; `printenv` prints one variable.',
    synopsis: ['env [OPTION]... [NAME=VALUE]... [COMMAND [ARG]...]', 'printenv [NAME]...'],
    desc: ['With no arguments `env` prints the **environment** (the exported variables), one `NAME=value` per line. With assignments and a COMMAND it runs the command with those variables added or changed, **without** changing your shell. `env -i` starts from an **empty** environment; `env -u NAME` removes one. `printenv NAME` prints just the value (nothing and status 1 if unset), which is handy in scripts. `env` is also what makes the line `#!/usr/bin/env bash` find `bash` through the `PATH`.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-i || --ignore-environment', desc: 'Start with an empty environment.' },
      { flag: '-u NAME || --unset=NAME', desc: 'Remove NAME from the environment of the command.' },
      { flag: '-0 || --null', desc: 'End each output line with NUL.' },
      { flag: '-C DIR || --chdir=DIR', desc: 'Change to DIR before running.' },
      { flag: '-S STRING', desc: 'Split STRING into arguments (shebang use: `#!/usr/bin/env -S bash -e`).' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'One variable', cmd: 'export GREETING=hello\nprintenv GREETING\nprintenv NOPE; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Search the environment', cmd: 'export PROJECT=demo\nenv | grep "^PROJECT="' },
      { title: 'A variable for one command', cmd: 'env MODE=test bash -c \'echo "mode: $MODE"\'\necho "after: [$MODE]"' },
      { title: 'A clean environment', cmd: 'env -i bash -c \'echo "HOME=[$HOME] PATH=[$PATH]"\'', note: 'Without `PATH` bash uses a default one. `env -i` is how to test a script without your usual setup.' },
      { title: 'Remove one variable', cmd: 'export SECRET=x\nenv -u SECRET bash -c \'echo "[$SECRET]"\'' },
      { title: 'Count the exported variables', cmd: 'env | wc -l | grep -qE "^[0-9]+$" && echo "a number"' },
      { title: 'Locate the interpreter portably', cmd: 'printf \'#!/usr/bin/env bash\\necho "hello from $(basename "$BASH")"\\n\' > s.sh\nchmod +x s.sh\n./s.sh', note: 'The shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash` finds `bash` wherever it is installed.' },
      { title: 'Shell variables are not in env', cmd: 'plain=1\nenv | grep -c "^plain="\nexport plain\nenv | grep -c "^plain="' },
    ],
    exit: ['0 on success; with a COMMAND, its status; 125 `env` failed, 126 cannot run, 127 not found.'],
    see: ['export', 'variables', 'set'],
  },
  {
    name: 'declare', kind: 'builtin', aliases: ['typeset', 'declare -i', 'declare -a', 'declare -A', 'declare -r', 'declare -x', 'declare -p', 'declare -l', 'declare -u', 'declare -n', 'readonly', 'local', 'declare -g', 'declare -f', 'declare -F'],
    summary: 'Declare variables with attributes: integer, array, associative array, read-only, exported, lower/upper case, reference.',
    synopsis: ['declare [-aAfFgilnrtux] [-p] [NAME[=VALUE]]...', 'readonly NAME=VALUE', 'local NAME=VALUE        # inside functions'],
    desc: ['`declare` creates variables and gives them **attributes**; a bare `NAME=value` already works for ordinary variables, but attributes let the shell help you: **`-i`** integer (assignments are evaluated as arithmetic), **`-a`** indexed array, **`-A`** associative array (bash 4+; a *must* before using string keys), **`-r`** read-only, **`-x`** exported, **`-l` / `-u`** convert the value to lower / upper case, **`-n`** name reference. A **minus** turns an attribute on, a **plus** turns it off. Inside a function `declare` makes the variable **local** (so does `local`); `-g` forces global. `readonly NAME` is `declare -r`. `declare -p NAME` prints how a variable is defined; `declare -f` / `-F` list functions.'],
    options: [
      { flag: '-i', desc: 'Integer: assignments are evaluated arithmetically.' },
      { flag: '-a', desc: 'Indexed array.' },
      { flag: '-A', desc: 'Associative array (dictionary).' },
      { flag: '-r', desc: 'Read-only.' },
      { flag: '-x', desc: 'Export to the environment.' },
      { flag: '-l / -u', desc: 'Always lower case / upper case.' },
      { flag: '-n', desc: 'Name reference: the variable is an alias for another variable.' },
      { flag: '-g', desc: 'Global even inside a function.' },
      { flag: '-p [NAME]', desc: 'Print the declaration of NAME (or all).' },
      { flag: '-f / -F', desc: 'Show functions (with / without the body).' },
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Integer variables compute', cmd: 'declare -i n=2+3\necho "$n"\nn=n*4\necho "$n"\ns=2+3\necho "$s"', note: 'Without `-i`, `s=2+3` is the text `2+3`.' },
      { title: 'Read-only', cmd: 'declare -r LIMIT=10\nLIMIT=20\necho "status: $?"\necho "$LIMIT"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Associative array', cmd: 'declare -A age\nage[ana]=30\nage[luis]=25\necho "${age[ana]}"\nfor k in "${!age[@]}"; do echo "$k=${age[$k]}"; done | sort', note: 'Without `declare -A` the keys would be evaluated as numbers (all 0).' },
      { title: 'Upper and lower case', cmd: 'declare -u shout=hello\necho "$shout"\ndeclare -l quiet=HELLO\necho "$quiet"' },
      { title: 'See how a variable is defined', cmd: 'declare -a list=(a b)\ndeclare -i n=5\ndeclare -x e=1\ndeclare -p list n e' },
      { title: 'Local variables in a function', cmd: 'x=global\nf() { local x=local; echo "inside: $x"; }\nf\necho "outside: $x"' },
      { title: 'Without local the function changes the global', cmd: 'x=global\ng() { x=changed; }\ng\necho "$x"' },
      { title: 'Name reference', cmd: 'target=5\ndeclare -n ref=target\nref=99\necho "$target"' },
      { title: 'Function listing', cmd: 'hello() { echo hi; }\ndeclare -F hello\ndeclare -f hello' },
      { title: 'Print every attribute of arrays', cmd: 'arr=(one "two words")\ndeclare -p arr' },
    ],
    see: ['variables', 'arrays', 'export', 'functions'],
  },
  {
    name: 'quoting', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['quotes', 'single quotes', 'double quotes', 'backslash', 'escape', 'escaping', '\\', "'...'", '"..."', '$\'...\'', "$'...'", 'ansi-c quoting', 'quote', 'quoting rules', 'comillas', 'single vs double quotes', 'quoting variables', '"$@"', '"$var"', 'unquoted', 'word splitting and quoting', 'quote removal', '\\$', '\\n', 'literal', 'literal characters', 'special characters'],
    summary: 'Quotes (`\'...\'`, `"..."`) and backslashes: how to stop the shell from interpreting special characters.',
    synopsis: ["'literal text'        # nothing is expanded", '"text with $var and $(cmd)"   # variables and commands are expanded', '\\CHARACTER          # protect one character', "$'tab:\\there\\n'     # C-style escapes"],
    desc: [
      'The shell gives **special meaning** to many characters: space (separates words), `$`, `*`, `?`, `[`, `~`, `!`, `` ` ``, `|`, `&`, `;`, `<`, `>`, `(`, `)`, `{`, `}`, `\\`, `#`, `"` and `\'`. **Quoting** removes that meaning so the characters reach the command as plain text.',
      '- **Single quotes** `\'...\'`: everything inside is **literal**; no expansion of any kind. A single quote cannot appear inside (end the quote, add `\\\'` or `"\'"`, restart: `\'it\'\\\'\'s\'`).\n- **Double quotes** `"..."`: the text is protected from word splitting and globbing, but **`$var`, `$(cmd)`, `$((expr))` and `` `cmd` ``** are still expanded, and the backslash still works before `$`, `` ` ``, `"`, `\\` and newline. (`!` also triggers history expansion in interactive shells.)\n- **Backslash** `\\c`: protects the **next single character** (`\\$`, `\\*`, `\\ ` for a space, `\\"`); at the end of a line it continues the command on the next line.\n- **ANSI-C quotes** `$\'...\'`: single-quoted text in which `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\\\`, `\\\'`, `\\xHH`, `\\u00e9` are interpreted.',
      'Quotes can be **mixed and concatenated** in one word: `"it\'s ${n} o\'clock"\'$\'`. Quote **removal** is the last step, so the command never sees the quotes. The golden rule: **always double-quote variable expansions** (`"$var"`, `"$@"`, `"$(cmd)"`) unless you really want splitting and globbing.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'What each quoting form allows', table: [
        ['Text', '`$var`', '`$(cmd)`', '`*` glob', '`\\n` escape', 'word splitting'],
        ['`unquoted`', 'yes', 'yes', 'yes', 'no (`\\n` is `n`)', 'yes'],
        ['`"double"`', 'yes', 'yes', 'no', 'no', 'no'],
        ['`\'single\'`', 'no', 'no', 'no', 'no', 'no'],
        ['`$\'ansi\'`', 'no', 'no', 'no', 'yes', 'no'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Single vs double quotes', cmd: 'name=Ana\necho \'Hello $name\'\necho "Hello $name"\necho Hello $name' },
      { title: 'Spaces separate words unless quoted', cmd: 'echo one   two\necho "one   two"\ntouch "my file"; ls\nls my file; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Globs are expanded unless quoted', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\necho *.txt\necho "*.txt"\necho \'*.txt\'' },
      { title: 'Command substitution in double quotes', cmd: 'echo "Today has $(printf "%s" 24) hours"\necho \'Today has $(printf "%s" 24) hours\'' },
      { title: 'Backslash protects one character', cmd: 'echo \\$HOME costs \\$5\necho "She said \\"hi\\""\necho a\\ b' },
      { title: 'A dollar sign in double quotes', cmd: 'echo "price: \\$5 and a tilde ~ stays"\necho "cost: $5"', note: '`$5` is the (empty) fifth positional parameter: use `\\$` or single quotes.' },
      { title: 'A single quote inside single quotes', cmd: 'echo \'it\'\\\'\'s fine\'\necho "it\'s fine"', note: 'Easier: use double quotes when the text has an apostrophe and no `$`.' },
      { title: 'ANSI-C quotes: tabs and newlines', cmd: 'echo $\'column1\\tcolumn2\\nline 2\'\nprintf "%s\\n" $\'a\\tb\' | od -c | head -n 1' },
      { title: 'Echo and the escapes', cmd: 'echo "a\\tb"\necho -e "a\\tb"', note: 'Plain `echo` prints the backslash-t; `echo -e` interprets it (printf always does).' },
      { title: 'Continuation lines', cmd: 'echo one \\\n  two \\\n  three' },
      { title: 'Mixing styles in one word', cmd: 'v=world\necho "hello $v"\' & \'"bye $v"', note: 'The pieces are glued into a single word.' },
      { title: 'Quote the special characters', cmd: 'echo "a;b" \'a&b\' a\\|b "a>b" # these are all plain text\ntouch "a;b"; ls', note: 'Without quotes, `;` `&` `|` `>` are shell operators.' },
      { title: 'Quoting empty values', cmd: 'v=""\nset -- $v; echo "unquoted: $# argument(s)"\nset -- "$v"; echo "quoted: $# argument(s)"', note: 'An unquoted empty variable disappears; a quoted one stays as an empty argument.' },
      { title: 'Quote "$@"', cmd: 'set -- "a b" c\nfor x in $@; do echo "[$x]"; done\nfor x in "$@"; do echo "[$x]"; done', note: '`"$@"` keeps each argument as it was; `$@` re-splits them.' },
      { title: 'A name that starts with a dash', cmd: 'touch -- -x\nrm -- -x; ls | wc -l', note: 'Not quoting but similar: `--` ends the options.' },
    ],
    notes: ['A missing closing quote makes the shell wait with the `>` prompt (the continuation prompt): press `Ctrl-C`.', 'Inside double quotes **`!`** can still trigger history expansion in an interactive shell (`echo "wow!"` may fail with `event not found`): use single quotes.', 'Regex patterns for `grep`/`sed`/`awk` go in single quotes so that the shell leaves `$ * [ ]` alone.'],
    see: ['expansions', 'variables', 'glob', 'word-splitting', 'echo', 'printf'],
  },
  {
    name: 'brace-expansion', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['braces', '{a,b}', '{1..5}', '{a..z}', '{01..10}', '{1..10..2}', 'brace expansion', 'sequence expression', 'brace range', 'expansion de llaves', '{,}', 'file{1,2,3}', 'dir/{a,b}/x', 'mkdir -p {a,b}/{c,d}', 'cp file{,.bak}'],
    summary: 'Brace expansion: generate several words from a pattern like `{a,b,c}` or `{1..10}`.',
    synopsis: ['PREFIX{item1,item2,...}SUFFIX', '{START..END[..STEP]}', '{a..z}   {01..10}'],
    desc: ['**Brace expansion** is performed by the shell **first** of all expansions, as pure text generation: it does not look at files. `{a,b,c}` becomes three words; text before and after (the *preamble* and *postscript*) is attached to each (`file{1,2}.txt` → `file1.txt file2.txt`). A **sequence** `{1..5}` produces numbers (also decreasing, with a **step** `{1..10..3}`, and with **leading zeros** `{01..10}`) or letters `{a..e}`. Several braces **combine** as a cartesian product (`{a,b}{1,2}` → `a1 a2 b1 b2`), and they nest. There must be **no spaces** inside the braces and at least one comma or `..` (otherwise they are literal). Braces cannot contain variables in a sequence (`{1..$n}` does not work: they are expanded before variables).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'A list', cmd: 'echo {a,b,c}\necho file{1,2,3}.txt\necho {red,green}-{small,large}' },
      { title: 'Numbers, letters, steps', cmd: 'echo {1..5}\necho {5..1}\necho {a..e}\necho {0..20..5}\necho {01..05}' },
      { title: 'Create a directory structure', cmd: 'mkdir -p project/{src,docs,tests}/{old,new}\nfind project | sort' },
      { title: 'Create many files', cmd: 'touch report_{jan,feb,mar}.txt log{1..3}\nls' },
      { title: 'Backup a file: cp file{,.bak}', cmd: 'echo data > config\ncp config{,.bak}\nls', note: '`{,.bak}` expands to the empty string and `.bak`, so the command is `cp config config.bak`.' },
      { title: 'Rename with the extension changed', cmd: 'touch notes.txt\nmv notes.{txt,md}\nls' },
      { title: 'Not a glob: no files needed', cmd: 'echo {a,b}.nosuch *.nosuch', note: 'Braces always expand; the glob stays literal when there is no match.' },
      { title: 'No variables in sequences', cmd: 'n=3\necho {1..$n}\nseq -s " " 1 $n\nfor i in $(seq 1 $n); do echo -n "$i "; done; echo', note: 'Use `seq` or a C-style `for` loop with variables.' },
      { title: 'Quoted or with spaces: literal', cmd: 'echo "{a,b}" \'{1..3}\' {a, b}' },
      { title: 'Combine with a loop', cmd: 'for d in {mon,tue,wed}; do echo "day: $d"; done' },
      { title: 'Nested', cmd: 'echo {a,b{1,2},c}' },
    ],
    see: ['expansions', 'glob', 'mkdir', 'seq', 'for'],
  },
  {
    name: 'parameter-expansion', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['${var:-default}', '${var:=default}', '${var:+alt}', '${var:?error}', '${var#pattern}', '${var##pattern}', '${var%pattern}', '${var%%pattern}', '${var/pat/rep}', '${var//pat/rep}', '${#var}', '${var:offset:length}', '${var^^}', '${var,,}', '${!var}', '${var-default}', '${var#prefix}', '${var%suffix}', '${var%.*}', '${var##*/}', '${var:-}', '${#v}', '${#var}', '${var:?}', '${var^}', '${var,}', '${!prefix*}', '${var@Q}', '${var:1}', '${var: -3}', 'substring', 'default values', 'string manipulation', 'expansión de parámetros', '${var:-word}', '${var:+word}', '${var:?word}', '${var:=word}', '${var#pattern}', '${var/#pat/rep}', '${var/%pat/rep}', '${var%pattern}', '${var##*:}', '${var#*:}', 'default value', 'remove prefix', 'remove suffix'],
    summary: 'The `${...}` forms: default values, string length, substrings, removing prefixes/suffixes, search and replace, case conversion.',
    synopsis: ['${var:-default}  ${var:=default}  ${var:+alt}  ${var:?message}', '${#var}  ${var:OFFSET:LENGTH}', '${var#PAT}  ${var##PAT}  ${var%PAT}  ${var%%PAT}', '${var/PAT/REP}  ${var//PAT/REP}  ${var^^}  ${var,,}'],
    desc: [
      '`${name...}` can do much more than read a variable, **without starting any external program** (faster and safer than `sed`/`cut`/`basename` for simple cases). `PAT` patterns are **globs** (`*`, `?`, `[ ]`), not regular expressions.',
      '- **Defaults**: `${v:-d}` the value of `v`, or `d` if `v` is unset **or empty**; `${v-d}` only if **unset**; `${v:=d}` also **assigns** `d` to `v`; `${v:+a}` gives `a` only if `v` is set and non-empty (else empty); `${v:?msg}` aborts the script with the message if `v` is unset or empty.\n- **Length and substrings**: `${#v}` the number of characters; `${v:3}` from position 3 (0-based); `${v:3:2}` two characters from 3; negative offsets count from the end and need a space or parentheses (`${v: -3}`).\n- **Cutting**: `${v#pat}` removes the **shortest** match of `pat` from the **start**; `${v##pat}` the **longest**; `${v%pat}` / `${v%%pat}` the shortest / longest from the **end**. Memory aid: `#` is on the left of `$` on the keyboard (prefix), `%` on the right (suffix).\n- **Replace**: `${v/pat/rep}` the first match, `${v//pat/rep}` all, `${v/#pat/rep}` only at the start, `${v/%pat/rep}` only at the end.\n- **Case**: `${v^^}` upper, `${v,,}` lower, `${v^}` / `${v,}` only the first character.\n- **Indirection**: `${!name}` the value of the variable whose *name* is stored in `name`. For arrays see `arrays`.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'Quick reference', table: [
        ['Form', 'Result'],
        ['`${v:-d}`', 'value of v; `d` if unset or empty'],
        ['`${v:=d}`', 'same, and assigns `d` to v'],
        ['`${v:+a}`', '`a` if v is set and not empty, else nothing'],
        ['`${v:?msg}`', 'error `msg` and exit if v is unset or empty'],
        ['`${#v}`', 'length'],
        ['`${v:o:n}`', 'n characters from offset o'],
        ['`${v#p}` `${v##p}`', 'remove shortest / longest prefix matching p'],
        ['`${v%p}` `${v%%p}`', 'remove shortest / longest suffix matching p'],
        ['`${v/p/r}` `${v//p/r}`', 'replace first / all'],
        ['`${v^^}` `${v,,}`', 'upper / lower case'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Default values', cmd: 'unset name\necho "Hello, ${name:-stranger}"\nname=Ana\necho "Hello, ${name:-stranger}"\nname=""\necho "[${name:-empty counts as unset}] [${name-only unset counts}]"' },
      { title: 'Assign a default (:=)', cmd: 'unset dir\necho "${dir:=/tmp/work}"\necho "now dir is $dir"' },
      { title: 'A default for an argument', cmd: 'set -- ""\nfile=${1:-input.txt}\necho "$file"\nset -- data.csv\nfile=${1:-input.txt}\necho "$file"' },
      { title: 'Alternative value (:+)', cmd: 'v=set\necho "${v:+--verbose}"\nunset v\necho "[${v:+--verbose}]"', note: 'Typical use: add an option only if a variable is set.' },
      { title: 'Required variable (:?)', cmd: 'unset TARGET\n( echo "${TARGET:?TARGET must be set}" ) 2>&1; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'Length', cmd: 'word=bash\necho "${#word}"\necho "${#HOME}" | grep -cE "^[0-9]+$"' },
      { title: 'Substrings', cmd: 's=abcdefghij\necho "${s:3}"\necho "${s:3:4}"\necho "${s: -3}"\necho "${s:0:${#s}-1}"', note: 'Offsets start at 0. A negative offset needs the space (`: -3`).' },
      { title: 'Remove an extension (suffix)', cmd: 'f=archive.tar.gz\necho "${f%.gz}"\necho "${f%.*}"\necho "${f%%.*}"', note: '`%.gz` removes that exact suffix; `%.*` removes the shortest match of `.*` (the last extension); `%%.*` the longest (everything from the first dot).' },
      { title: 'Get the extension / the file name (prefix)', cmd: 'f=/home/ana/docs/report.final.txt\necho "${f##*.}"\necho "${f##*/}"\necho "${f%/*}"', note: '`${f##*/}` is `basename`, `${f%/*}` is `dirname`, `${f##*.}` the extension.' },
      { title: 'Remove a prefix', cmd: 'url=https://example.com/page\necho "${url#https://}"\nline="key=value=more"\necho "${line#*=}"\necho "${line##*=}"', note: '`#*=` removes up to the first `=`; `##*=` up to the last.' },
      { title: 'Replace', cmd: 'p="the cat and the hat"\necho "${p/the/a}"\necho "${p//the/a}"\necho "${p/#the/A}"\necho "${p/%hat/dog}"\necho "${p//[aeiou]/_}"' },
      { title: 'Delete (replace by nothing)', cmd: 's="a-b-c"\necho "${s//-}"\necho "${s/-}"' },
      { title: 'Case conversion', cmd: 'w="hello World"\necho "${w^^}"\necho "${w,,}"\necho "${w^}"' },
      { title: 'Rename many files with expansion', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt c.txt\nfor f in *.txt; do mv "$f" "${f%.txt}.md"; done\nls' },
      { title: 'Indirection', cmd: 'colour=blue\nref=colour\necho "${!ref}"' },
      { title: 'Pattern is a glob, not a regex', cmd: 'f=photo_2024_01.jpg\necho "${f#*_}"\necho "${f%_*}"\necho "${f//[0-9]/#}"' },
      { title: 'Join the result with other text', cmd: 'v=world\necho "Hello, ${v^}! (${#v} letters)"' },
    ],
    notes: ['Always quote: `"${f%.txt}.md"`.', 'Expansion is performed by bash itself: no external process. For anything needing regular expressions use `sed` or `[[ =~ ]]`.', 'The `:` in `:-`, `:=`, `:+`, `:?` means "unset **or empty**"; without it only "unset".'],
    see: ['variables', 'arrays', 'basename', 'dirname', 'sed', 'expansions'],
  },
  {
    name: 'command-substitution', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['$(...)', '$( )', '$(cmd)', '$(command)', '`cmd`', 'backticks', '$(func)', '$()', 'command substitution', 'sustitución de comandos', '$(( ))vs$( )', 'subshell output', '$(< file)', '$(cat file)', '$(cmd) in quotes'],
    summary: '`$(command)`: replace the expression by the standard output of the command.',
    synopsis: ['$(COMMAND)       # preferred', '`COMMAND`         # old style (backticks)', '$(< FILE)         # fast way to read a file'],
    desc: ['`$(command)` runs the command in a **subshell** and is replaced by whatever it printed on **standard output**, with **trailing newlines removed**. Use it to put output into a variable (`now=$(date +%F)`), into an argument (`ls -l $(which bash)`), into a string (`echo "I am $(whoami)"`). The old form with backticks `` `command` `` does the same but cannot be nested easily and the quoting rules are confusing; always prefer `$( )`. It can be nested: `$(basename $(pwd))`.', 'Rules of thumb: **quote it** (`"$(cmd)"`) unless you want word splitting of the result; the **exit status** of the substitution is that of the command (`x=$(false); echo $?` is 1) but is lost when the substitution is used inside another command (`echo $(false)`); only **stdout** is captured (add `2>&1` to catch errors); variables set inside it are **lost** (subshell). Don\'t confuse with `$(( ))` (arithmetic) and `<( )` (process substitution).'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Into a variable', cmd: 'files=$(printf "a\\nb\\nc\\n" | wc -l)\necho "number: $files"' },
      { title: 'Inside a string', cmd: 'echo "The year is $(date -d 2024-06-01 +%Y) and I am $(id -un)"' },
      { title: 'As arguments', cmd: 'touch a.txt b.txt\nls -l $(ls *.txt) | wc -l\nwc -l < <(ls)' },
      { title: 'Nested', cmd: 'mkdir -p x/y\ncd x/y\necho "I am in $(basename "$(dirname "$(pwd)")")/$(basename "$(pwd)")"' },
      { title: 'Trailing newlines are removed', cmd: 'x=$(printf "text\\n\\n\\n")\necho "[$x]"' },
      { title: 'Quotes keep the lines together', cmd: 'x=$(printf "a b\\nc d\\n")\necho $x\necho "$x"' },
      { title: 'Only stdout is captured', cmd: 'out=$(ls /nonexistent 2>&1)\necho "captured: $out"\nout=$(ls /nonexistent 2>/dev/null)\necho "captured: [$out]"' },
      { title: 'The exit status', cmd: 'x=$(false); echo "assignment status: $?"\necho $(false); echo "inside echo: $?"' },
      { title: 'Read a whole file', cmd: 'printf "line 1\\nline 2\\n" > f\ncontent=$(< f)\necho "$content"', note: '`$(< file)` is the fast form of `$(cat file)`.' },
      { title: 'Variables set inside are lost', cmd: 'x=$(y=5; echo "inside: $y"); echo "$x"\necho "outside: [$y]"' },
      { title: 'Use in a condition or a loop', cmd: 'if [ "$(wc -l < /etc/passwd)" -gt 3 ]; then echo "many users"; fi\nfor f in $(seq 1 3); do echo -n "$f "; done; echo' },
      { title: 'Backticks (old style)', cmd: 'echo "Today: `date -d 2024-06-01 +%F`"', note: 'Works, but `$( )` nests and quotes much better.' },
      { title: 'A function\'s output', cmd: 'greet() { echo "hello, $1"; }\nmsg=$(greet Ana)\necho "[$msg]"', note: 'Functions "return" text through stdout and numbers through `return`.' },
    ],
    see: ['variables', 'arithmetic', 'process-substitution', 'quoting', 'expansions'],
  },
  {
    name: 'arithmetic', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['$(( ))', '$((...))', '(( ))', '((...))', 'let', 'arithmetic expansion', 'arithmetic $(( ))', 'integer arithmetic', 'aritmética', '((i++))', 'i++', '$((a+b))', 'operators', 'modulo', '%', '**', 'let i++', 'arithmetic evaluation', 'arithmetic for', 'for (( ))', '(( ))vs[[ ]]'],
    summary: 'Integer arithmetic in the shell: `$(( ))`, `(( ))`, `let` (and `expr`, `bc` for the rest).',
    synopsis: ['$(( EXPRESSION ))     # expands to the value', '(( EXPRESSION ))       # command: status 0 if the value is non-zero', 'let "EXPRESSION"', 'expr 2 + 3        echo "scale=2; 10/4" | bc'],
    desc: [
      '**`$(( expr ))`** computes an **integer** expression and is replaced by the result. Variables inside need **no `$`** (`$((a + b))`). Operators as in C: `+ - * / %` (remainder), `**` (power), `++ --`, `+= -= *= /= %=`, comparison `< <= > >= == !=` (result 1 or 0), logic `&& || !`, bitwise `& | ^ ~ << >>`, ternary `c ? a : b`. Bases: `0x1F` hex, `010` **octal** (a classic trap: `09` is an error), `2#101` binary.',
      '**`(( expr ))`** is a *command*: it evaluates the expression and its **exit status is 0 (true) if the result is not zero**, so it is perfect for conditions (`if (( n > 3 ))`) and for assignments / increments without printing (`(( i++ ))`, `(( total += x ))`). `let "i=i+1"` does the same. The C-style loop is `for (( i=0; i<5; i++ ))`.',
      '**Limits**: only **integers** (division truncates: `7/2` is 3; `1/3*3` is 0), 64-bit; no floating point. For decimals use `bc` (`echo "scale=2; 10/3" | bc`) or `awk`. The external `expr` works too but needs escaped operators (`expr 2 \\* 3`) and is only needed in POSIX `sh` scripts.',
    ],
    sections: [{
      title: 'Operators', table: [
        ['Operator', 'Meaning', 'Example → result'],
        ['`+ - * /`', 'add, subtract, multiply, integer divide', '`$((7/2))` → `3`'],
        ['`%`', 'remainder (modulo)', '`$((7%3))` → `1`'],
        ['`**`', 'power', '`$((2**10))` → `1024`'],
        ['`++` `--`', 'increment / decrement', '`((i++))`'],
        ['`+=` `-=` `*=`', 'update', '`((n *= 2))`'],
        ['`<` `<=` `>` `>=` `==` `!=`', 'comparison → 1 or 0', '`$((3 > 2))` → `1`'],
        ['`&&` `||` `!`', 'logic', '`((a > 0 && b > 0))`'],
        ['`& | ^ ~ << >>`', 'bit operations', '`$((1 << 4))` → `16`'],
        ['`c ? a : b`', 'conditional', '`$((n > 0 ? 1 : -1))`'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'The basics', cmd: 'echo $((2 + 3 * 4))\necho $(( (2 + 3) * 4 ))\necho $((10 / 3)) $((10 % 3)) $((2 ** 8))' },
      { title: 'Variables without $', cmd: 'a=7; b=3\necho "$((a + b)) $((a - b)) $((a * b)) $((a / b)) $((a % b))"' },
      { title: 'Integer division truncates', cmd: 'echo $((7 / 2))\necho $((-7 / 2))\necho $((1 / 3 * 3))' },
      { title: 'Update a variable', cmd: 'n=5\nn=$((n + 1)); echo $n\n((n++)); echo $n\n((n *= 2)); echo $n\nlet "n -= 3"; echo $n' },
      { title: 'A condition with (( ))', cmd: 'n=7\nif (( n % 2 == 0 )); then echo even; else echo odd; fi\n(( n > 5 )) && echo "big"\n(( n > 10 )) || echo "not that big"' },
      { title: 'Exit status of (( ))', cmd: '(( 3 > 2 )); echo "true: $?"\n(( 2 > 3 )); echo "false: $?"\n(( 0 )); echo "zero: $?"\n(( 5 )); echo "nonzero: $?"', fails: true, note: 'The result **0 is false**, anything else is true (the opposite of an exit status of 0 meaning success).' },
      { title: 'Counting in a loop', cmd: 'count=0\nfor f in a b c; do ((count++)); done\necho "$count"\ntotal=0\nfor n in 1 2 3 4; do total=$((total + n)); done\necho "$total"', note: 'Careful: `((count++))` returns status 1 the first time (value 0): fatal under `set -e`; use `count=$((count+1))` there.' },
      { title: 'C-style for', cmd: 'for (( i=1; i<=5; i++ )); do echo -n "$i "; done; echo\nfor (( i=10; i>0; i-=3 )); do echo -n "$i "; done; echo' },
      { title: 'Bases', cmd: 'echo $((0x1F)) $((010)) $((2#1010))\necho $((16#ff))\nprintf "%d %x %o\\n" 255 255 255', note: 'A leading `0` means **octal**: `$((08))` is an error.' },
      { title: 'Comparisons give 1 or 0', cmd: 'echo $((5 > 3)) $((5 < 3)) $((5 == 5))\nx=15; echo $(( x >= 10 && x <= 20 ))' },
      { title: 'Ternary and bit operators', cmd: 'n=-4\necho $(( n < 0 ? -n : n ))\necho $((1 << 3)) $((12 & 10)) $((12 | 3)) $((12 ^ 5))' },
      { title: 'Random number in a range', cmd: 'r=$(( RANDOM % 6 + 1 ))\n(( r >= 1 && r <= 6 )) && echo "dice between 1 and 6"' },
      { title: 'Division by zero', cmd: 'echo $((1 / 0))', fails: true, note: 'An arithmetic error aborts the whole command line (in a script, the script).' },
      { title: 'Not floating point: bc and awk', cmd: 'echo $((10 / 4))\necho "scale=2; 10 / 4" | bc\nawk \'BEGIN {printf "%.2f\\n", 10 / 4}\'' },
      { title: 'expr (POSIX, external)', cmd: 'expr 2 + 3\nexpr 6 \\* 7\nexpr 10 / 3 \\+ 1\nexpr length "hello"\nexpr 5 \\> 3', note: 'In `expr` operators like `*` and `>` must be escaped; spaces around operators are required.' },
      { title: 'Arithmetic needs integers', cmd: 'x=abc\necho $((x + 1))\ny=2.5\necho $((y + 1))', fails: true, note: 'A non-numeric word is read as a variable name (empty = 0); a decimal is a syntax error.' },
    ],
    notes: ['In `$(( ))` variables can be written with or without `$`; inside `[ ]` and `[[ ]]` use `-lt`, `-gt` for numbers, or just use `(( ))`.', 'Under `set -e`, `(( expr ))` that evaluates to 0 **kills the script**: write `(( expr )) || true`.', 'There is no overflow warning: 64-bit wrap-around.'],
    see: ['variables', 'test', 'for', 'bc', 'expr', 'declare'],
  },
  {
    name: 'word-splitting', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['IFS', '$IFS', 'IFS=', 'IFS=: read', 'IFS= read -r', 'splitting', 'field splitting', 'IFS=, read -ra', 'word splitting', 'division de palabras'],
    summary: 'Word splitting and `$IFS`: how unquoted expansions are cut into words, and how to split on other characters.',
    synopsis: ['IFS=$\' \\t\\n\'   (default)', 'IFS=: read -r a b c <<< "x:y:z"', 'IFS=, read -ra array <<< "a,b,c"'],
    desc: ['After parameter, command and arithmetic expansion, the shell takes every **unquoted** result and splits it into words at the characters of the variable **`IFS`** (the *Internal Field Separator*). Its default is **space, tab and newline**, and runs of them count as one separator. That is why `ls $file` breaks when the name contains a space and why you quote: **`"$var"` is never split**. Splitting does not happen on the literal text you type, only on expansion results.', 'Changing `IFS` lets you split on other characters, most often with `read`: **`IFS=: read -r a b c`** splits one line at colons (the assignment applies only to that command). An **empty** `IFS=` disables trimming and splitting (`IFS= read -r line` keeps leading and trailing spaces: the correct way to read lines). Never leave a changed `IFS` set for the rest of the script: put it in a subshell or on the command.'],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Unquoted: split', cmd: 'v="a b   c"\nset -- $v\necho "$# words"\nset -- "$v"\necho "$# word"' },
      { title: 'Unquoted: also globbed', cmd: 'touch x.c y.c\nv="*.c"\nfor f in $v; do echo "[$f]"; done\nfor f in "$v"; do echo "[$f]"; done' },
      { title: 'Split a line into variables', cmd: 'IFS=: read -r user pass uid rest <<< "root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash"\necho "$user has uid $uid"\necho "rest: $rest"', note: 'The last variable receives all that is left.' },
      { title: 'Split into an array', cmd: 'IFS=, read -ra parts <<< "red,green,blue"\necho "${#parts[@]} parts: ${parts[1]}"' },
      { title: 'Keep leading and trailing spaces', cmd: 'echo "   padded   " | { read -r a; echo "[$a]"; }\necho "   padded   " | { IFS= read -r a; echo "[$a]"; }', note: 'With the default IFS, `read` trims blanks; `IFS= read -r` is the way to read a line exactly.' },
      { title: 'Change IFS safely', cmd: 'data="a:b:c"\n( IFS=:; set -- $data; echo "$# fields, second: $2" )\necho "IFS is unchanged: ${#IFS} characters (space, tab, newline)"', note: 'The parentheses keep the change in a subshell.' },
      { title: 'Join with IFS ("$*")', cmd: 'set -- a b c\n( IFS=,; echo "$*" )\necho "$*"', note: '`"$*"` joins the arguments with the first character of IFS.' },
      { title: 'Empty IFS: no splitting at all', cmd: 'v="a b c"\nIFS= ; set -- $v; echo "$# word"; IFS=$\' \\t\\n\'' },
      { title: 'Why a loop over $(ls) is fragile', cmd: 'touch "a b" c\nfor f in $(ls); do echo "[$f]"; done\nfor f in *; do echo "[$f]"; done', note: 'The first splits the name with a space; the glob does not.' },
      { title: 'Lines, not words', cmd: 'printf "one two\\nthree\\n" > f\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do echo "[$line]"; done < f\nmapfile -t lines < f; echo "${#lines[@]} lines"' },
    ],
    see: ['quoting', 'read', 'expansions', 'arrays', 'special-parameters'],
  },
  {
    name: 'arrays', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['array', 'associative arrays', 'associative array', '${arr[@]}', '${#arr[@]}', '${!arr[@]}', 'arr=()', 'arr+=()', 'mapfile', 'readarray', 'indexed arrays', 'arrays: ${arr[@]}', '${arr[*]}', '${arr[0]}', 'array slice', '"${arr[@]}"', 'keys of an array', 'arrays asociativos'],
    summary: 'Bash arrays: indexed (`arr=(a b c)`) and associative (`declare -A map`), with all the ways to read them.',
    synopsis: ['arr=(a b c)   arr[3]=d   arr+=(e f)', '"${arr[@]}"   "${#arr[@]}"   "${!arr[@]}"   "${arr[@]:1:2}"', 'declare -A map; map[key]=value; "${map[key]}"; "${!map[@]}"'],
    desc: [
      'An **indexed array** holds a list of strings addressed by number starting at **0**. Create it with `arr=(one two "three four")` or element by element (`arr[0]=one`), add with `arr+=(five)`, read an element with `${arr[1]}`. The expansions to know (always **in double quotes**): **`"${arr[@]}"`** every element, each as a separate word (the correct way to loop or pass them on); `"${arr[*]}"` all as a single string joined by the first character of IFS; **`${#arr[@]}`** the number of elements; **`${!arr[@]}`** the list of indices (or keys); `${arr[@]:start:count}` a slice; `${#arr[i]}` the length of one element; `"${arr[@]/pat/rep}"` an operation on every element.',
      'An **associative array** (bash 4+) maps **string keys** to values: it must be declared with **`declare -A`** first. `map[key]=value`, `${map[key]}`, `"${!map[@]}"` (the keys; in no particular order), `${#map[@]}` (count). They are the tool for counting and grouping (`count[$word]++`). `unset arr[i]` removes an element (the others keep their index); `unset arr` the whole array. `mapfile -t arr < file` (or `readarray`) reads the lines of a file into an array; `read -ra arr <<< "a b c"` splits a line into an array.',
    ],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Create and read', cmd: 'colours=(red green blue)\necho "${colours[0]} ${colours[2]}"\necho "${colours[@]}"\necho "${#colours[@]} elements"' },
      { title: 'Loop over all elements', cmd: 'files=("my file.txt" "other.txt" "third one.md")\nfor f in "${files[@]}"; do echo "[$f]"; done', note: 'The quotes keep the names with spaces whole.' },
      { title: '@ versus *', cmd: 'a=("x y" z)\nfor i in "${a[@]}"; do echo "[$i]"; done\nfor i in "${a[*]}"; do echo "[$i]"; done\nfor i in ${a[@]}; do echo "[$i]"; done' },
      { title: 'Add and change elements', cmd: 'a=(one two)\na+=(three four)\na[1]=TWO\necho "${a[@]} (${#a[@]})"' },
      { title: 'Indices and slices', cmd: 'a=(a b c d e)\necho "${!a[@]}"\necho "${a[@]:1:3}"\necho "${a[@]: -2}"\necho "${a[-1]}"' },
      { title: 'Remove an element', cmd: 'a=(a b c d)\nunset "a[1]"\necho "${a[@]}"\necho "${!a[@]}"', note: 'The index 1 is gone; the others are not renumbered.' },
      { title: 'Array from a command / a file', cmd: 'printf "ana\\nluis\\nbea\\n" > names\nmapfile -t people < names\necho "${#people[@]} people, last: ${people[-1]}"\nfiles=(*)\necho "${files[@]}"', note: '`files=(*)` is the safe way to store file names.' },
      { title: 'Split a string into an array', cmd: 'read -ra words <<< "the quick brown fox"\necho "${#words[@]} words, second: ${words[1]}"\nIFS=, read -ra f <<< "a,b,c"; echo "${f[2]}"' },
      { title: 'Associative array', cmd: 'declare -A capital\ncapital[spain]=Madrid\ncapital[france]=Paris\ncapital[italy]=Rome\necho "${capital[france]}"\necho "${#capital[@]} countries"\nfor k in "${!capital[@]}"; do echo "$k: ${capital[$k]}"; done | sort' },
      { title: 'Count occurrences', cmd: 'declare -A count\nfor w in apple pear apple fig pear apple; do ((count[$w]++)); done\nfor w in "${!count[@]}"; do echo "$w ${count[$w]}"; done | sort' },
      { title: 'Is a key present?', cmd: 'declare -A m=([a]=1 [b]=2)\n[[ -v m[a] ]] && echo "a exists"\n[[ -v m[z] ]] || echo "z does not exist"\necho "${m[z]:-default}"' },
      { title: 'Operations on every element', cmd: 'a=(apple banana cherry)\necho "${a[@]^^}"\necho "${a[@]/a/_}"\necho "${#a[1]}"' },
      { title: 'Pass an array to a command', cmd: 'args=(-l -a)\ntouch x\nls "${args[@]}" x | wc -l\nopts=()\nopts+=(-1)\nls "${opts[@]}" x' },
      { title: 'Without declare -A keys are numbers', cmd: 'notassoc[a]=1\nnotassoc[b]=2\necho "${#notassoc[@]} element(s): ${notassoc[0]}"', note: 'The keys `a` and `b` are evaluated as the variable names (value 0): both write to index 0.' },
    ],
    notes: ['`$arr` alone is just `${arr[0]}`.', 'Always write `"${arr[@]}"` with quotes.', 'Arrays are a bash feature (not in POSIX `sh`/dash).', 'Index `-1` (last element) works in bash 4.3+.'],
    see: ['declare', 'parameter-expansion', 'read', 'for', 'word-splitting'],
  },
  {
    name: 'special-parameters', kind: 'syntax', aliases: ['$#', '$@', '$*', '$?', '$$', '$!', '$0', '$1', '$_', '$-', '"$*"', 'positional parameters', 'special variables', '$1 $2', '${10}', '$9', 'parámetros posicionales', 'arguments', 'script arguments', 'script argument', 'command line arguments', '$FUNCNAME', '$LINENO', '$BASH_SOURCE', '$1..$9', '${1:-default}', '"$#"', 'argv', 'positional'],
    summary: 'Positional and special parameters: `$0` `$1`.. `$#` `$@` `$*` `$?` `$$` `$!` `$_`.',
    synopsis: ['$0 $1 $2 ... ${10}      $#      "$@"  "$*"      $?  $$  $!  $_  $-'],
    desc: ['The shell sets these parameters automatically; you can read them but not assign them (except with `set --` / `shift`):'],
    sections: [{
      title: 'The parameters', table: [
        ['Parameter', 'Meaning'],
        ['`$0`', 'the name of the script as it was called (in a function still the script)'],
        ['`$1` `$2` ... `${10}`', 'the positional parameters: the arguments of the script or function; two digits need braces'],
        ['`$#`', 'the **number** of arguments'],
        ['`"$@"`', 'all arguments, **each as a separate word** (the one to use)'],
        ['`"$*"`', 'all arguments as **one word**, joined by the first character of `IFS`'],
        ['`$?`', 'exit status of the last command (0 = success)'],
        ['`$$`', 'PID of the current shell'],
        ['`$!`', 'PID of the last background command'],
        ['`$_`', 'last argument of the previous command'],
        ['`$-`', 'the current shell options (letters)'],
        ['`$PPID`', 'PID of the parent'],
        ['`shift [N]`', 'discards the first N arguments: `$2` becomes `$1`...'],
        ['`set -- a b c`', 'replaces the positional parameters'],
      ],
    }],
    examples: [
      { title: 'Arguments of a script', cmd: 'cat > args.sh << \'EOF\'\n#!/bin/bash\necho "script: $0"\necho "first: $1, second: $2"\necho "count: $#"\necho "all: $@"\nEOF\nbash args.sh apple "red pear" fig' },
      { title: '"$@" vs "$*"', cmd: 'set -- "a b" c "d e"\nprintf "[%s]" "$@"; echo\nprintf "[%s]" "$*"; echo\nprintf "[%s]" $@; echo', note: '`"$@"` keeps three arguments; `"$*"` is one string; unquoted `$@` re-splits.' },
      { title: 'Loop over the arguments', cmd: 'set -- one two "three four"\nfor arg in "$@"; do echo "arg: $arg"; done\nfor arg; do echo "again: $arg"; done', note: '`for arg` with no `in` iterates over `"$@"`.' },
      { title: 'Count and check', cmd: 'cat > need2.sh << \'EOF\'\nif [ $# -ne 2 ]; then echo "usage: $0 SRC DST" >&2; exit 1; fi\necho "copy $1 to $2"\nEOF\nbash need2.sh a; echo "status: $?"\nbash need2.sh a b; echo "status: $?"', fails: true },
      { title: 'shift: process the arguments one by one', cmd: 'set -- a b c d\nwhile [ $# -gt 0 ]; do echo "got $1 ($# left)"; shift; done' },
      { title: 'More than nine arguments', cmd: 'set -- a b c d e f g h i j k\necho "$10 ${10} ${11}"', note: '`$10` is `$1` followed by a 0.' },
      { title: 'Default for a missing argument', cmd: 'set --\necho "name: ${1:-nobody}"\nset -- Ana\necho "name: ${1:-nobody}"' },
      { title: '$? is the status of the last command', cmd: 'true; echo "$?"\nfalse; echo "$?"\nls /nonexistent 2>/dev/null; echo "$?"\necho "$?"', note: 'The last `echo "$?"` prints 0: the status of the previous `echo`.' },
      { title: 'Use $? right away', cmd: 'grep -q root /etc/passwd\nstatus=$?\necho "grep returned $status"' },
      { title: '$$ and $!', cmd: 'echo "$$" | grep -cE "^[0-9]+$"\nsleep 1 &\necho "$!" | grep -cE "^[0-9]+$"\nwait' },
      { title: '$_ : the last argument', cmd: 'mkdir newdir\ncd "$_" && pwd' },
      { title: '$0 and the script name', cmd: 'cat > who.sh << \'EOF\'\necho "I am $0 ($(basename "$0"))"\nEOF\nbash who.sh\nbash ./who.sh', note: '`$0` is how the script was called; `basename "$0"` strips the path.' },
      { title: 'Set the positional parameters yourself', cmd: 'set -- x y z\necho "$# $2"\nset -- $(echo one two)\necho "$1-$2"' },
      { title: 'Pass the arguments on', cmd: 'cat > wrap.sh << \'EOF\'\nprintf "[%s]" "$@"; echo\nEOF\nbash wrap.sh "a b" c\nbash wrap.sh', note: '`"$@"` passes arguments exactly as received (and nothing if there are none).' },
      { title: 'Option letters of the shell: $-', cmd: 'echo "$-" | grep -q h && echo "hashing is on"\nset -u; echo "$-" | grep -o u', note: '`set -u` adds `u` to `$-`.' },
    ],
    see: ['variables', 'shift', 'getopts', 'exit-status', 'functions', 'quoting'],
  },
]);
